syntax = "proto3";

package devalbo.dlc.std.v1;

option go_package = "github.com/devalbo/devalbo-ilc/dlc-platform/gen/go/devalbo/dlc/std/v1;dlcstdv1";

// ===========================================================================
// DLC STANDARD — vendored. Do not edit this copy.
//
//   source:  dlc-platform/proto/devalbo/dlc/std/v1/version.proto
//   refresh: make sync-std-proto
//   verify:  make verify-std-proto   (CI fails when a copy has drifted)
// ===========================================================================

// WHICH VERSION OF THE DLC STANDARD SET AN APP WAS BUILT AGAINST.
//
// # The hole this fills
//
// The standard protos are COPIED into every app — `make sync-std-proto` vendors
// them, and CI holds every in-repo copy byte-identical to the source. That works
// exactly as far as this repo reaches. An app built somewhere else could carry a
// six-month-old `status.proto`, send values from a vocabulary that has since
// moved, and nothing anywhere would notice.
//
// This is the same question `schema_id` answers about an app's own messages,
// asked one layer up about the vocabulary they are written in.
//
// # Why an ENUM and not a version string
//
// Because the point is ORDER. `SchemaInfo.version` is free text, deliberately —
// an app versions itself however it likes and a platform cannot police that. But
// a host asking "is this app older than the vocabulary I speak" needs to
// COMPARE, and free text cannot be compared: "1.10" against "1.9" is a parser
// and a convention, and a hash can only ever say same-or-different.
//
// A declared enum is an ordered, closed set. `>` works. This is the one idea
// worth taking from TypeSpec's versioning library, where a version is an enum
// the schema is projected through rather than a string somebody parses.
//
// # Why the version travels with the copy
//
// An app reports the value ITS vendored copy declares. That is the whole trick:
// an old copy declares an old maximum, so an app cannot claim a version of the
// vocabulary it has never seen — the answer is a property of the bytes it was
// compiled with rather than a number somebody typed.
enum StdVersion {
  STD_VERSION_UNSPECIFIED = 0;

  // The first published set: `status.proto` (`StatusLevel`) and this file.
  STD_VERSION_V1 = 1;
}

// # There is deliberately no `STD_VERSION_LATEST`
//
// One was written, as an `allow_alias` duplicate of the newest value, so an app
// could report "whatever my copy knows" without naming a version. `buf lint`
// refused it, and the rule is right: an alias means two names decode to one
// value, and an enum whose values are not distinct is one a reader cannot
// round-trip.
//
// So an app names the version explicitly. That is a visible line to change when
// the vendored set is refreshed — which reads worse and behaves better than a
// symbol that silently starts meaning something else. In-repo, staleness is
// caught anyway: `make verify-std-proto` holds every copy byte-identical to the
// source, and the scaffold golden lists the file with its hash.
